export default "Partners do not need to track the menstrual cycle to understand it. The practical value of cycle awareness (knowing when to offer more support, when to schedule demanding commitments, when to expect lower energy) does not require data access. It requires understanding a biological pattern and communicating about it.\n\nThe line between support and surveillance is not always obvious, and it matters in practice. In healthy relationships, it is a useful distinction to think through. In relationships with power imbalances or coercive dynamics, it is a safety consideration.\n\n## What Partners Benefit From Knowing\n\nThe menstrual cycle is not simply a period and everything else. It involves four distinct phases with measurably different hormonal environments. Each phase produces predictable effects on energy, mood, and cognition.\n\n**Menstrual phase** (approximately days 1-5): Both estrogen and progesterone are at their lowest. Energy is typically reduced. Many people experience cramping, fatigue, and lower capacity for high-demand tasks. This is a physiologically quiet phase, not a malfunction.\n\n**Follicular phase** (days 1-14, overlapping with menstrual): Estrogen rises as follicles develop. The mid-to-late follicular phase, roughly days 7-13 in a 28-day cycle, tends to be associated with higher energy, improved mood, and greater sociability. This is a reasonable time for demanding social plans or high-effort collaborative projects.\n\n**Ovulatory phase** (around day 14): Peak estrogen, accompanied by an LH surge and testosterone peak. The ovulatory window is often associated with higher confidence, social engagement, and physical energy. A 2013 study in Hormones and Behavior documented measurable differences in self-assessed attractiveness and sociability around ovulation.\n\n**Luteal phase** (days 15-28): Progesterone rises and then falls. The early luteal phase is often comfortable. The late luteal phase (days 22-28) is when PMS and PMDD symptoms concentrate. Progesterone has sedating and mood-modulating effects. Its withdrawal before menstruation is associated with mood changes, reduced energy, increased sensitivity to criticism, and cognitive slowing that many people describe as brain fog.\n\nUnderstanding this pattern does not require access to a tracking app. It requires knowing where in the cycle a partner currently is, information that can be shared in conversation, and adjusting expectations and plans accordingly.\n\n## The Communication Approach\n\nPartners who want to offer practical support do not need data. They need communication from the person doing the tracking.\n\nA partner who knows \"she's in her late luteal phase this week\" has everything they need to plan around it. They do not need app access to verify it, do not need to see the historical chart, and do not need to track it independently. The person doing the tracking shares what is relevant. The partner responds to that information.\n\nThis structure keeps the data with the person it belongs to. It also means the information shared is what the tracking person has chosen to share, not an unfiltered log of cycle history, fertility status, and symptoms that a partner accesses on their own.\n\nPractical applications: avoid scheduling high-demand social plans in the pre-period window; do not treat late-luteal cognitive slowing as a performance issue or a character flaw; recognize that early-cycle energy changes are physiological, not mood-related. None of these require app access. They require understanding the cycle and listening when someone describes where they are in it.\n\n## Where Support Becomes Surveillance\n\nThe transition from support to surveillance happens when one person's cycle data is accessible to another without explicit, ongoing, revocable consent.\n\nThe access routes are not always obvious.\n\n**Shared app accounts**: If two people share login credentials to a period app, or if one person sets up the account on a shared device, the non-tracking partner has full access to cycle history, symptom logs, fertility data, and any conditions disclosed.\n\n**Family Apple ID or Google account**: A shared family Apple ID or a Google account used across multiple household devices can create indirect access to period app data through iCloud backup, keychain sync, or shared app subscriptions. If the app auto-logs in on a family device, anyone with access to that device can view the data.\n\n**Cloud backup**: Period app data backed up to iCloud or Google Drive is accessible to anyone with the associated account credentials, including, in some configurations, a partner's Apple family plan or shared Google account.\n\nThe National Domestic Violence Hotline's Safety NET program (documented in their 2014 report on technology safety and intimate partner violence, updated periodically since) identifies period app monitoring as a form of reproductive surveillance. Period app data has been used to track a partner's schedule and location, to monitor fertility and conception intent, and to document reproductive health information without consent.\n\n## The Consent Checklist\n\nThree questions determine whether cycle data access is consensual:\n\n1. Does the person whose cycle is being tracked know the other person has access?\n2. Did they actively grant that access, not by omission or by default?\n3. Can they revoke it independently, without requiring the other person's cooperation or awareness?\n\nIf any answer is no, the access is not consensual. \"She never told me not to\" is not consent. \"We share the family plan\" is not consent. \"I set it up for her\" is not consent.\n\nConsensual sharing looks like: \"I invited my partner to see my app data, they have their own login, and I can remove their access at any time in the app's sharing settings.\" Most mainstream apps offer partner sharing features with this structure, including explicit invitation and revocation mechanisms.\n\n## The Partner as Privacy Stakeholder\n\nOne aspect of period app privacy that is rarely discussed: if a partner's name, relationship status, sexual activity, or identity appears in a period app log, they have privacy interests in that data too.\n\nMany period apps encourage logging sexual activity, partner information, contraception method, and fertility intent. If a person's partner is named in these logs, or if the log implicitly records the partner's involvement in conception attempts, contraception decisions, or sexual health, then a breach, subpoena, or data sale affects more than one person.\n\nThis is not an argument against logging. Detailed logs are valuable for health tracking. It is an argument that conversations about what to log, what to disclose, and where data is stored are conversations that involve everyone whose information appears in them.\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nFloriva's local-first architecture changes the access control problem entirely. Data is stored only on the device of the person who logged it. There is no readable central record to share, no family plan to accidentally expose data through, and no cloud backup containing cycle history. A partner cannot access Floriva data without physical access to the unlocked device. That is an access method that is visible, obvious, and impossible to disguise as a background sharing feature. This does not make all decisions about cycle sharing irrelevant. It does mean that sharing decisions are explicit and deliberate, not the product of app architecture you did not fully examine when you signed up.";
